  "raw": {
    "pillar": "Science & Health Breakthroughs",
    "headline": "Can a simple change to your sleep habits boost your grades? This study says yes.",
    "challenge": "College students are notoriously sleep-deprived, with many struggling to meet the recommended 7 hours per night. Poor sleep can lead to lower academic performance and even mental health issues.",
    "solution": "A recent study found that offering small cash incentives and personalized bedtime reminders helped students sleep more, leading to better grades. The intervention increased sleep by 19 minutes per night during the treatment period.",
    "resultHeading": "Students improved their grades by 0.075–0.089 points",
    "resultLine": "The effects persisted even after the incentives ended — check out the full study at the link in bio.",
    "whyMatters": "This study shows that improving sleep can be a cost-effective way to boost academic performance, especially for freshmen and STEM students who often struggle with time management and demanding coursework.",
    "engagementQuestion": "How much do you think sleep impacts your daily performance?",
    "captionLead": "A new study reveals that a simple change to sleep habits can directly boost academic performance. Here's how it works!",
    "storyHashtag": "SleepResearch",
    "sources": [
      "https://www.sciencealert.com/a-simple-change-to-sleep-directly-boosts-academic-performance-study-reveals",
